(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-supraventricular-arrhythmias-quiz"]={"title":"Arrhythmias I: Supraventricular Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which drug is used for persistent symptoms from premature junctional contractions?","choices":["Procainamide","Metoprolol","Adenosine","Atropine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If symptoms from premature junctional contractions persist, a beta blocker such as metoprolol is used if not contraindicated; reassurance of the benign rhythm is often adequate. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What is the usual prognosis of inappropriate sinus tachycardia?","choices":["Most need a pacemaker","Usually benign","High risk of sudden death","Progressive heart failure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Inappropriate sinus tachycardia usually has a benign prognosis, which is why it is generally left untreated once secondary causes are excluded. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Sick sinus syndrome with symptomatic bradycardia is treated with which?","choices":["Catheter ablation","Adenosine","Permanent pacemaker","Atropine as needed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When symptoms correlate with bradycardia in sick sinus syndrome, a permanent pacemaker is placed; tachy-brady syndrome also often needs one. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is the first-line treatment for stable supraventricular tachycardia?","choices":["Intravenous procainamide","A vagal maneuver","Adenosine","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stable supraventricular tachycardia is treated first with a vagal maneuver, then adenosine (which may be repeated once); an unstable patient gets immediate synchronized cardioversion. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A slow sinus rhythm that is causing hypotension and confusion is treated first with which drug?","choices":["Metoprolol","Digoxin","Atropine","Adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable bradycardia is treated first with atropine, then a temporary pacemaker if it fails; chronic symptomatic disease needs a permanent pacemaker. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"In tachy-brady syndrome, which tachyarrhythmia is most common?","choices":["Ventricular tachycardia","Sinus tachycardia","Atrial fibrillation","Atrial flutter"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In tachy-brady syndrome a tachyarrhythmia, most commonly atrial fibrillation, alternates with symptomatic bradycardia or with offset pauses when the fast rhythm stops. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which drug is a direct oral anticoagulant preferred in nonvalvular atrial fibrillation?","choices":["Warfarin","Aspirin","Procainamide","Apixaban"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Apixaban is one of the direct oral anticoagulants preferred in nonvalvular atrial fibrillation, with dabigatran, edoxaban and rivaroxaban. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which therapy is usually curative for focal atrial tachycardia?","choices":["Repeated adenosine","A permanent pacemaker","Atropine","Catheter ablation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Catheter ablation of the ectopic focus is usually curative for focal atrial tachycardia, in about 90%; oral beta blockers or calcium channel blockers are the initial chronic therapy. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"An educated patient stops an episode of nodal reentry at home with a vagal maneuver. What is the usual advice?","choices":["Start lifelong anticoagulation","Arrange a pacemaker","Urgent care is not usually needed","Go to the emergency department"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. If a vagal maneuver terminates nodal reentry in a patient who has been educated, urgent medical attention is usually not required; episodes are usually well tolerated. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which is a recognized cause of sick sinus syndrome?","choices":["Surgical injury to the node","Excess thyroid hormone","Anemia from blood loss","Heavy caffeine intake"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Causes of sick sinus syndrome include sinoatrial node ischemia, injury to the node from cardiac surgery, electrolyte abnormalities, medications and hypothyroidism. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is the main management of multifocal atrial tachycardia?","choices":["Adenosine twice","Synchronized cardioversion","Treat the underlying condition","Catheter ablation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Management of multifocal atrial tachycardia is treating the underlying condition, such as decompensated lung disease or heart failure; in hospital a short course of rate control may be added. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"In an older adult with new symptomatic bradycardia, which review is a key first step?","choices":["The medication list","A family history of fainting","A test for accessory pathways","An exercise tolerance history"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers, nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers and digoxin cause sinus bradycardia and sick sinus syndrome, so the medication list is reviewed and treating the underlying cause comes first. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How is inappropriate sinus tachycardia usually managed?","choices":["Usually left untreated","Lifelong beta blocker","Synchronized cardioversion","Daily oral amiodarone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Inappropriate sinus tachycardia is generally left untreated: patients tolerate the symptoms better than the treatment, beta blockers are poorly tolerated, and the prognosis is usually benign. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"On the CHA2DS2-VASc (stroke risk) score, which result is low risk that may not need anticoagulation?","choices":["0 in a man or 1 in a woman","2 in a man or 3 in a woman","3 in a man or 4 in a woman","Any score under 5"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A score of 0 for men or 1 for women is low risk and may not require anticoagulation; patients above that typically need it unless bleeding risk is high. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"On standard electrocardiogram paper, how much time does one small box represent?","choices":["0.20 seconds","0.12 seconds","1.00 second","0.04 seconds"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Thin lines are 1 mm apart and each small box is 0.04 seconds; five small boxes make one large box of 0.20 seconds, and 25 small boxes make one second. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What is the definitive management of atrial flutter?","choices":["Adenosine","A pacemaker","Vagal maneuvers","Ablation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ablation is the definitive management of atrial flutter, which has a substantial recurrence rate and is harder to rate-control than fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"In sinus arrest, which rhythm may take over?","choices":["Atrial flutter","Ventricular fibrillation","Multifocal atrial tachycardia","A junctional rhythm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In sinus arrest the sinus node does not generate an impulse, and a junctional rhythm may take over, often with inverted (retrograde) P waves or none visible. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"In sinus node exit block, what happens to the impulse?","choices":["Never generated by the node","Blocked at the atrioventricular node","Replaced by a ventricular focus","Generated, but it cannot leave the node"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In sinus node exit block the sinus node generates the impulse but it does not exit to the atria, so a P wave and its beat are missing; in sinus arrest the impulse is never generated. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Besides drugs and thyroid disease, which condition is a listed cause of sinus bradycardia?","choices":["Intracranial hypertension","Pulmonary embolism","Pain or anxiety","Hypoxia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Increased intracranial pressure is a listed cause of sinus bradycardia, together with sinus node ischemia, late-stage infection, hypothyroidism and the rate-slowing drugs. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Persistent, symptomatic premature atrial contractions are treated first with which?","choices":["A beta blocker","Adenosine","Procainamide","Amiodarone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Persistent, symptomatic premature atrial contractions are treated with a beta blocker such as metoprolol if not contraindicated, or ablation when they are frequent or trigger atrial fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Before outpatient cardioversion of atrial flutter, how long is anticoagulation given?","choices":["7 days","6 months","21 days","48 hours"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Outpatient cardioversion of atrial flutter (and fibrillation) is done after 21 days of anticoagulation, and anticoagulation is part of flutter management. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Adenosine slows a tachycardia and reveals atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White. Which drug is used?","choices":["A second dose of adenosine","Intravenous atropine","Intravenous magnesium","Intravenous procainamide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. If Wolff-Parkinson-White is known, or slowing from adenosine reveals atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White, intravenous procainamide is used; adenosine and other nodal blockers leave the accessory pathway unopposed. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"How do most people with sinus bradycardia present?","choices":["With neck pulsations","With exertional chest pain","Without symptoms","With syncope at rest"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Most people with sinus bradycardia are asymptomatic, and an asymptomatic sinus bradycardia is not treated. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"How do most people with focal atrial tachycardia describe it?","choices":["Neck pulsations (frog sign)","Abrupt-onset palpitations","Syncope during exercise","Fatigue over months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Most people with focal atrial tachycardia report abrupt-onset palpitations, and it is generally a benign arrhythmia. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Stable atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response is treated how?","choices":["Intravenous atropine","Intravenous rate control","Synchronized cardioversion","Immediate ablation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A hemodynamically stable patient in rapid ventricular response gets intravenous rate control; a stable patient without it gets rate control by mouth. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which rhythm describes atrial fibrillation?","choices":["Irregularly irregular, no P waves","Regular, with sawtooth waves","Irregular, three P-wave shapes","Regular, with hidden P waves"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atrial fibrillation is irregularly irregular with absent P waves and no PR interval, usually at 110 to 160 with a narrow QRS. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What does the HAS-BLED (bleeding risk) score estimate?","choices":["Need for a permanent pacemaker","Major bleeding risk on anticoagulation","Stroke risk in atrial fibrillation","Chance of returning to sinus rhythm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The HAS-BLED (bleeding risk) score estimates the risk of major bleeding in a patient on anticoagulation, to weigh risk against benefit in atrial fibrillation care. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What does a premature atrial contraction look like?","choices":["An early, abnormal P wave","An early wide QRS, no P wave","Inverted P, narrow QRS","A sawtooth baseline"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A premature atrial contraction begins in the atria and shows an early, abnormally shaped P wave followed by a narrow QRS. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which supraventricular tachycardia is irregularly irregular?","choices":["Atrial flutter with 2:1 block","Atrial fibrillation","Focal atrial tachycardia","Nodal reentrant tachycardia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Atrial fibrillation is irregularly irregular with absent P waves; most other supraventricular tachycardias, such as nodal reentry and focal atrial tachycardia, are regular. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"How does atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia usually terminate?","choices":["Intravenous procainamide","Vagal maneuvers or adenosine","Synchronized cardioversion","Catheter ablation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nodal reentry almost always terminates with vagal maneuvers or adenosine; synchronized cardioversion is rarely required, and episodes are usually well tolerated. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 41"}]};
